        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;height:100%;overflow:auto;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#eef4fb;font-family:system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#182d4c'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:6px solid #294b83;border-bottom:1px solid #182d4c;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>PRIMITIVE · portal</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:850 clamp(36px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.035em;text-wrap:balance;color:#182d4c'>Portal</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.4 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#46617f'>An opening from one graph into another graph’s interface.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Meaning</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>An addressed opening that consumes or follows another graph’s exposed interface. Encoding: a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>portal</code> node with a target and source provenance.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A Portal is where one graph appears in another. It shows the target graph’s outside face: one of the faces the target offers, chosen for the room the Portal has. Going through it puts the visitor on the target’s ground, or in the graph as a whole when it has none.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A Portal is movement: it takes you to a graph or node. It does not bring anything into the graph you are in. Including a piece of a node is the Fragment&#39;s job.</p></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #182d4c'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#182d4c'>At a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #a6bed9'><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Node type</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>portal</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Shown when browsing</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Yes</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Encoded as</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>a portal node with a target and source provenance</dd></dl></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Invariants</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A Portal names another artifact without acquiring its authorship. Following, opening alongside and projecting a graph must preserve target identity and source provenance. A missing target is a resolution failure, not permission to invent a replacement node.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Contract</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Current data includes target.ref, target.mode, target.portId and target.surfaceId; sourceRef/sourceNodeId/sourcePayload remain supported consumer fields. Durable addresses are tlz:// or supported graph URLs. A request for a node or a Port named in the address (after <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>#</code>) must resolve against the addressed artifact.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A Portal&#39;s authority is one of navigate, consume-view, bridge and runtime, in that order, and starts at navigate. Naming a face to draw (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>sourcePayload</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surfaceId</code>) raises it to consume-view. Bridge and runtime are never inferred from what the target offers or from having a face: they are only what the Portal itself states, and the graph&#39;s own policy can only lower the result.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Ports and Relationships</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Target Port exposure and the ground determine entry. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>root</code> refers to the graph’s root, its implicit interface. Portal navigation and the authority an import is granted are separate relationships.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Behavior</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>graphNavigationAddress resolves public exposure and the ground. workspaceCapabilities and the workspace loader open or focus target graph instances. A Page Route acts as a Portal in the graph: a click on it sends the same navigation request a Portal does (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>utils/pageRoute.js</code>). A Portal only moves you. It has no expand mode; including a piece of a node is the Fragment&#39;s job.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Known gaps</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Mode support depends on the actual navigation caller; do not claim an arbitrary target.mode works everywhere. The Surface host integration task records remaining non-default host selection gaps.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A Portal’s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>target.mode</code> is stored by older graphs but changes nothing the editor offers, and no behaviour is known to depend on it.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Representations</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>The Portal can present the target’s exposed representation through the client’s resolver; its carrier type and the target’s semantic type are distinct. Rendering does not transfer mutation authority.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A Portal is edited on its own card. It has one address, which may end in a colon and the name of an exposed Port. Under the address the editor says what it opens: the graph’s name and description, and, when the graph exposes Ports, a row of chips to open onto one. The chips are a convenience; the Port can just as well be typed after the colon. The face a Portal shows is the entrance’s own, worked out for the room it has, so there is nothing to choose. A target that cannot be loaded is reported, not replaced. Its authority is worked out from what it names, so the editor does not set it.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Every Portal has an Enter button, shown when the pointer is over it, so going through never depends on what the face is doing. A click and a double-click go through as the navigation scheme describes.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Not an import’s grant, not a face, and not an instruction to normalize or rewrite the target graph.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Not a Fragment. A Portal goes somewhere; a Fragment includes a piece of the node you are in.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Examples</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>The Portal under Examples, Back to the primitives, is a real Portal node: click it and the list of every primitive opens.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Try it:</p><ol style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:26px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Switch to edit mode (the pencil).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Press Add node and choose Portal. A card appears.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Select it and press Edit, or double-click it. Give it the address of the graph it opens onto. Under the address the editor says what it opens. If the graph exposes Ports, choose one to open onto it; that is optional.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Switch to browse mode. The Portal shows the face of what it opens. Hover over it and press Enter, or click it as the navigation scheme describes.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>The other graph opens. A Portal only takes you there: nothing from it comes into the graph you are in.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>A missing destination is reported, and no replacement is invented.</li></ol><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>No example graph is linked from this page yet.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>On the Root page, the Weekend Reading Group route is a Page Route that acts as a Portal: a click opens that graph.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Where this lives</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Source (twilite repository):</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/graphNavigationAddress.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/workspaceCapabilities.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/hooks/useGraphWorkspace.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/pageRoute.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/portalEditing.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/Nodes/PortalInlineEditor.js</code></li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Focused tests (twilite repository):</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/graphNavigationAddress.test.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/portalLegacyAnchorValidation.test.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/renderedPortalType.test.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/pageRoute.test.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/portalEditing.test.js</code></li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>These pointers identify evidence to inspect. Structural validation does not prove runtime or browser behavior.</p></section></div></div></div>"
        },
