    {
      "id": "portal-representations",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Representations",
      "position": {
        "x": 430,
        "y": 200
      },
      "width": 720,
      "height": 380,
      "data": {
        "title": "Representations",
        "body": "The Portal can present the target’s exposed representation through the client’s resolver; its carrier type and the target’s semantic type are distinct. Rendering does not transfer mutation authority.\n\nA Portal is edited on its own card. It has one address, which may end in a colon and the name of an exposed Port. Under the address the editor says what it opens: the graph’s name and description, and, when the graph exposes Ports, a row of chips to open onto one. The chips are a convenience; the Port can just as well be typed after the colon. The face a Portal shows is the entrance’s own, worked out for the room it has, so there is nothing to choose. A target that cannot be loaded is reported, not replaced. Its authority is worked out from what it names, so the editor does not set it.\n\nEvery Portal has an Enter button, shown when the pointer is over it, so going through never depends on what the face is doing. A click and a double-click go through as the navigation scheme describes.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592072632,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "primitive-portal"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
